    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d418">

                <pb facs="0801" n="681" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg681"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/4185: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-01-09T19:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 9,
                                1922—7 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received January 9—2:57
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>11. New Cabinet reported in my telegram number 4, January 3, 10 p.m.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d418-f1"/> as without popular enthusiasm, encountering fierce
                    opposition from Wu Pei-fu,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d418-f2"/> hostile from the
                    beginning and now exacerbated by the Government’s pardoning his Anfuite enemies.
                    Wu in two circular telegrams appeals on popular issue of Shantung to Chinese
                    people North and South to condemn Liang Shih-yi<anchor n="3" xml:id="d418-f3"/>
                    for his subservience to Japan in willingness, so Wu charges, to accept Japanese
                    loan for redemption railway and having wired instructions to Chinese delegates
                    Washington to avert this, Wu declares, of all evils, selling one’s country is
                    greatest, and concludes telegram with appeal that Chinese people should rise up
                    and drive away their enemy and further wire to their Washington delegates urging
                    them to stick to their original decision of restoring railway to Chinese control
                    without any connection with the Japanese.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d418-f4"/></p>
                <p>Rumors that Wu is recalling troops from Hupeh.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d418-f1" n="1" xml:id="d418-f1-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Post</hi>, <ref target="#pg761">p. 761</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d418-f2" n="2" xml:id="d418-f2-content">
                <p>Inspector-General of Hupeh and Hunan.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d418-f3" n="3" xml:id="d418-f3-content">
                <p>Appointed Premier Dec. 14, 1921.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d418-f4" n="4" xml:id="d418-f4-content">
